  "fromId": "k_palatalized",
  "toId": "ts",
  "preamble": "The transformation of a palatalized voiceless velar plosive (kʲ) into a voiceless alveolar affricate (ts) is a well-attested historical sound change, primarily driven by the process of palatalization. This shift involves a forward movement of the articulation point and a change in the manner of articulation from a stop to an affricate. In Luwian the PIE palatovelar *ḱ is written ⟨z⟩, probably [ts] (Melchert), while plain *k stays k: *ḱḗr 'heart' > zārza, *ḱwṓ 'dog' > zuwan-.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Palatalization, Affrication, Assibilation",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Old Church Slavonic",
      "languageFamily": "East Slavic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "kʲ",
          "to": "ts",
          "note": "Palatalized k' changed to a 'ts' sound (written as 'c') before front vowels resulting from ancient diphthongs. For instance, the Proto-Balto-Slavic *káina 'price' became OCS cěna [tsěna]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Najdi Arabic",
      "languageFamily": "Semitic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "k",
          "to": "ts",
          "note": "In Najdi and related Gulf varieties, the velar stop /k/ affricates to [ts] in the vicinity of high front vowels. While the input is often described as /k/, the conditioning environment implies a palatalized allophone as an intermediate step."
        }
      ]
    },
